#:lesson{:id :lesson/grade6.patterns.shapes,
         :title "Patterns in Shapes",
         :grade-level 6,
         :domain :patterns,
         :concepts #{:concept/shape-sequence},
         :skills #{:skill/patterns.shape-properties},
         :blocks
         [#:block{:title "Patterns in Shapes",
                  :type :explain,
                  :body
                  "Other important and basic patterns that occur in Mathematics are patterns of shapes. These shapes may be in one, two, or three dimensions (1D, 2D, or 3D)—or in even more dimensions. The branch of Mathematics that studies patterns in shapes is called geometry."}
          #:block{:title "Shape Sequences",
                  :type :explain,
                  :body
                  "Shape sequences are one important type of shape pattern that mathematicians study.\nA sequence of Regular Polygons looks like:\nTriangle (3 sides), Quadrilateral/Square (4 sides), Pentagon (5 sides), Hexagon (6 sides), Heptagon (7 sides), Octagon (8 sides), Nonagon (9 sides), Decagon (10 sides)."}
          #:block{:title "Relation to Number Sequences",
                  :type :explain,
                  :body
                  "Often, shape sequences are related to number sequences in surprising ways. Such relationships can be helpful in studying and understanding both the shape sequence and the related number sequence.\n\nFor example, the number of sides in the shape sequence of Regular Polygons is given by the counting numbers starting at 3: \n3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10...\n\nThe word ‘regular’ refers to the fact that these shapes have equal-length sides and also equal ‘angles’."}
          #:block{:title "Practice Time",
                  :type :practice-ref,
                  :exercise-set-id
                  :exercise-set/grade6.patterns.shapes}]}
